# tests/utf8/octal_wide.rxt -- K125 (lane langfix2): an octal escape above
# `\377` (`\400`..`\777`, U+0100..U+01FF) is ACCEPTED under utf8, in a
# class and outside one, and as the octal reading of the backreference-vs-
# octal rule; it is refused (err 151 "octal value is greater than \377 in
# 8-bit non-UTF-8 mode") under byte only. The ceiling is the encoding's
# universe, the same range check braced `\x{...}` takes (pattern_language.md
# §5.2). pcrec refused the utf8 cells with the byte-mode diagnostic before
# the fix.
#
# EVERY EXPECTATION WAS PRODUCED BY libpcre2 10.46 at PCRE2_UTF (tests/lib/
# p2gen.py over p2probe). Outside a class a `\4`.. escape is module
# `backrefs`' digit row (pattern_language.md §7.3), so those blocks enable it.

# THE ADVERSARY'S CELL: `\400` is U+0100.
pattern \400
features backrefs
encoding utf8
m "\xc4\x80" 0 2
n "\xc4\x81"
n "a"

# The top of the three-digit range, U+01FF.
pattern \777
features backrefs
encoding utf8
m "\xc7\xbf" 0 2
n "\xc7\xbe"

# In a class.
pattern [\400]
encoding utf8
m "\xc4\x80" 0 2
n "a"

# A class range across the whole three-digit band above `\377`.
pattern [\400-\777]
encoding utf8
m "\xc5\xbf" 0 2
m "\xc4\x80" 0 2
m "\xc7\xbf" 0 2
n "\xc8\x80"
n "\xc3\xbf"

# Negated: matches any OTHER character, one whole character wide.
pattern [^\400]
encoding utf8
n "\xc4\x80"
m "\xc3\xa9" 0 2

# After a group: `\400` with one group is the OCTAL reading (rule 3: 4 > 1 group).
pattern (a)\400
features backrefs
encoding utf8
m "a\xc4\x80" 0 3
g 1 0 1
n "a"

# Quantified.
pattern \400+
features backrefs
encoding utf8
m "\xc4\x80\xc4\x80" 0 4
n "\xc4\x81"

# Caseless: U+0100 and U+0101 are a fold pair.
pattern \400
features backrefs,modifiers
encoding utf8
flags i
m "\xc4\x81" 0 2
m "\xc4\x80" 0 2

# CONTROL: the scan takes at most three digits -- `\1000` is `\100` then `0`.
pattern \1000
features backrefs
encoding utf8
m "@0" 0 2
n "@"

# CONTROL: under byte the same escape is still err 151 ...
pattern \400
features backrefs
perr

# ... and in a class.
pattern [\400]
perr

